From cb9f94d802a578fa5efb0827e3ad9465a891b524 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xuxh1 <18172956238@163.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 04:13:47 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(crop): sow the second rice season after the first-season
 harvest (C-95)

C-45 sows the second season of double-cropped irrigated rice on the
calendar date pdrice2 and skips it when the first season is still
growing on that date. In the 2-degree global run g2_o6 only 33-35 % of
the irrigated rice flagged as double-cropped got a second harvest, and
1 % in China, because:
- pdrice2 falls inside the model's first season: for 39 % of the flagged
  irrigated area it is less than 150 d after the first-season date;
- pdrice2 is an areal mean of day-of-year values: for 29 % of the flagged
  area the 0.5-degree dates straddle the year end and average to a
  mid-year date (the PH-RiF cell: 319-349 and 15-21 average to 206, 16 d
  after the first-season date 189);
- in China the calendar's second date lies in February-April, before the
  first-season date (55 % of the flagged Chinese area), so the second
  crop is sown in winter and cut on the first-season date before grain
  fill, with the paddy bunded from sowing.

New host key DEF_RICE_SECOND_SOW_DAYS (default -1, off). When set, the
second season is sown that many days (land preparation) after the
harvest of a first season, if the heat left before the first-season
date comes round again reaches the maturity requirement of the crop just
harvested. The heat is summed above the crop base temperature, as
gddplant is, over a sinusoidal annual cycle of 2 m air temperature
through annavg_tref and the warm-half-year mean from gdd020, so no new
state or restart field is needed. A crop not sown on the first-season
date is the second season and is still cut on that date if standing.
With the key set, the pre-season flooding of candidate 25 counts only
the first-season date.

With the key negative the C-45 branches run exactly as before and the
model is bit-for-bit unchanged.

